# 4. V-Model

## What is the V-Model?

The **V-Model (Verification and Validation Model)** is an SDLC model where every development phase has a corresponding testing phase. Testing activities are planned early, improving quality and reducing defects.

---

## Verification

Checks whether the software is being built correctly.

Examples:

* Requirement review
* Design review
* Code review

---

## Validation

Checks whether the correct software has been built.

Examples:

* Unit Testing
* Integration Testing
* System Testing
* Acceptance Testing

---

## V-Model Diagram

```text
Verification                      Validation

Requirement Analysis      ↔     Acceptance Testing

System Design             ↔     System Testing

Architecture Design       ↔     Integration Testing

Module Design             ↔     Unit Testing

            Coding
```

---

## Advantages of V-Model

* Early defect detection.
* Better planning.
* Improved software quality.
* Simple and easy to understand.
* Strong documentation.

---

## Disadvantages

* Less flexible for changing requirements.
* Costly if requirements change frequently.
* Not ideal for highly dynamic projects.

---

# 5. QA Integration Points in SDLC

Quality Assurance should be involved throughout the SDLC, not only during testing.

| SDLC Phase           | QA Activities                                             |
| -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| Requirement Analysis | Review requirements, identify ambiguities, prepare RTM    |
| Planning             | Estimate testing effort, define test strategy             |
| Design               | Review architecture, identify test scenarios              |
| Development          | Review code, prepare test cases, support unit testing     |
| Testing              | Execute tests, report defects, perform regression testing |
| Deployment           | Verify release, perform smoke testing                     |
| Maintenance          | Test bug fixes, perform regression testing                |

### Benefits of Early QA Involvement

* Finds defects earlier.
* Reduces development cost.
* Improves communication.
* Produces higher-quality software.
* Speeds up releases.

